(The `plugin_params.json` keys themselves are `Plugin` internals and out of scope; see the README for their defaults.)

## Edge cases and unusual behavior

- **First-path-wins on all dictionary singletons.** Because `get_constraints_dict`, `get_variables_dictionary`, and `get_binary_variables_ids_map` use function-local `static`, only the first path argument is honored; later calls return the same data regardless of their argument (`:50`, `:92`, `:153`). This is relevant because the framework may call these callbacks with different roots over the run [Confirmed].
- **`variables_dictionary.csv` path asymmetry.** `OnBendersStart` initializes the variables dictionary with `output_root` (`:184`) while `OnBendersMicroIterationEnd` asks for `input_root` (`:222`). Since `OnBendersStart` runs first, the dictionary is effectively read from `output_root/plugin_inputs/variables_dictionary.csv`, and the later `input_root` is ignored. If that file is expected under `input_root`, this is a latent bug [Confirmed behavior; "bug" characterization Inferred].
- **No header-row skipping.** The CSV parsers read every line as data (`:24`, `:63`, `:108`). If the runtime CSVs have headers, the first row becomes a bogus map entry (e.g. a "family name" of `"family_name"`). Whether headers exist in the real files is Unknown (files not committed) [Confirmed parsing behavior; Unknown for the data].
- **`constraints_dict[family_name]` is silent on missing keys.** In `OnBendersMicroIterationEnd` (`:237`), using `operator[]` means a `Plugin`-returned family absent from `constraints_dictionary.csv` contributes zero constraints with no error, unlike `.at()` which is used for the variables dictionary (`:229`) and would throw [Confirmed].
- **`check_if_constraints_family_added` auto-inserts unknown subproblems.** Its `operator[]` call (`:160`) creates an empty entry for any subproblem name not seen before [Confirmed].
- **`z_dict` skips silent master variables.** In `OnBendersMasterResolutionEnd`, investment variables absent from `master_out` produce no `z_dict` entry at all (`:273-275`). The `Plugin` therefore receives a partial decision map, not a full 0/1 vector [Confirmed].
- **Different clocks.** The micro-iteration callback times itself with `steady_clock` (`:219`, `:241`), the master callback with `high_resolution_clock` (`:277`, `:279`); both are converted to milliseconds [Confirmed].
- **`clean_added_constraints_families_per_sub` iterates one map, clears the other via `operator[]`** (`:142-147`). Safe only while both maps share exactly the same key set; they do in the current push-together flow, but a key present only in the bool map would never be cleared [Confirmed; safe-in-practice is Inferred].

## Impact on the task

The boundary file is purely orchestration: it owns no grid-modeling logic and no logging I/O logic. Its correctness hinges on three things the main agent should watch:

1. **Dictionary contracts.** The three CSVs must use exactly the positional layouts described above, with no header rows (or the parsers must be changed to skip them). The `variables_dictionary.csv` read-from-`output_root` asymmetry (`:184` vs `:222`) is the most likely source of a "flow key not found" `std::out_of_range` if the file only exists under `input_root`.
2. **Singleton path binding.** If the same process ever needs different input roots for different subproblems or iterations, the static singletons will return stale data; the design assumes one fixed root for the whole run.
3. **Deduplication trust boundary.** Deduplication is the only thing preventing the same constraint family from being injected twice into a subproblem. It depends on the per-sub lists being reset exactly once per Benders iteration (`OnBendersIterationStart`) and on `constraints_dict` containing every family the `Plugin` can return; a missing family silently degrades to "nothing added" with no diagnostic.

## Open questions

- **Do the runtime CSV files contain header rows?** The files are not committed, so this cannot be checked. The parsers do not skip headers, so a header would be ingested as data. A sample file or the generator script (`parse_micro_iterations.py`) would answer this.
- **Why is `get_master_resolution_start` defined but never used?** It is dead code within this file. It may be vestigial from an older design where `OnBendersMasterResolutionStart` stamped a timer; confirming would require git history.
- **What exactly does `broadcast_factors()` send?** Confirmed to exist and be called on all ranks (`:285`, `gridModelisation.h:508`), but its payload is `Plugin` internals and was intentionally not explored.
- **Is the `output_root` vs `input_root` asymmetry for the variables dictionary intentional?** The code reads from `output_root` at startup and `input_root` at micro-iteration time; only the former takes effect due to static init. Whether the runtime expects the file in `output_root` is unverified.

